# Primitives (docs layout)

These rules apply to the **default** primitives tree: **`docs/primitives/`**
beside a Rails app. Canonical workflow: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/maintaining-primitives/SKILL.md`**.
One-time setup: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/bootstrapping-primitives/SKILL.md`**.

The tree holds the four primitives that outlive generated code:

| Primitive | Where it lives |
|-----------|----------------|
| **Intent** — what the system must do and why | `intent:` clauses in each capability doc's YAML frontmatter |
| **Compilation** — architecture generated code compiles into | Generic Rails shape: this harness (compass + rules). App-specific: `compilation.md` |
| **Evaluations** — executable proof behaviour survived change | `evaluations:` on each clause, naming the RSpec files that prove it |
| **Provenance** — lineage of decisions and rejected alternatives | `## Provenance` section, append-only |

Intent and evaluations live in **frontmatter, not prose**, because they are the
two primitives a machine must read: **`agent_harness_rails evals`** checks that every active
clause on a `built` doc is proven and that every proof still points at a live
clause. Shape and provenance stay prose — they are for humans, and no parser
improves them.

## Layout (fixed — no other file types)

```
docs/primitives/
  SCHEMA.md          # Co-owned contract (agent + human)
  index.md           # One line per capability + status — the only entry point
  compilation.md     # App-wide constraints. Human-owned.
  capabilities/      # One file per capability: intent + evaluations in frontmatter,
                     # then ## Shape and ## Provenance
```

**No topic pages, synthesis pages, or hubs.** Content that does not answer
"what must this do / what constrains it / where is it proven / why is it this
way" has no home here — that scope discipline, not maintenance effort, is what
prevents sprawl.

## Reading contract

No agent loads more than **three files**: `index.md` + the **one** target
capability doc + `compilation.md`. Cross-links between capability docs are for
humans; no workflow requires following one. Do not read other capability docs
unless the work explicitly spans a second named capability. Sole exception:
the maintainer's **`lint`** operation is a health pass and reads the whole
tree by design.

### Finding the right capability

Three routes, in the order they are cheapest:

1. **From a spec** — the tagged example names its capability outright:
   `intent: "comment_threads#I2"`. If you are already in the suite, this is a
   read, not a search.
2. **From code, by grep** — `ls capabilities/ | grep comment`. Filenames carry
   the app's resource names and domain prefixes (§ Style), so a file under
   `app/models/` usually finds its doc in one command.
3. **From `index.md`** — the entry point, and the only route that works when the
   capability owns no single resource. One line per doc, so it stays a cheap read
   at any plausible number of capabilities.

Route 3 is also how you answer *"does this work span a second capability?"* —
which the contract above requires you to judge before opening another doc. That
makes the quality of each `index.md` line load-bearing rather than decorative:
a line that does not distinguish its capability from its neighbours is what
produces a duplicate doc, and duplication is far more expensive to unwind than
the line was to write.

## Ownership and write points

- **`compilation.md`** — **Human-owned.** Every agent that reads the tree
  reads it (the implementor deliberately reads nothing here — constraints
  reach it via excerpts pasted into its dispatch prompt), agents propose
  amendments as review findings, and **never edit it directly** (exception:
  the drafting step of `bootstrapping-primitives`, which ends with human edit).
- **`intent:` clauses** — Human-co-owned: clauses transcribe the user's answers
  to requirements-gate or brainstorm questions. Written or amended only by the
  planning workflows (`brainstorming-rails-omakase` seeds,
  `writing-rails-plans` confirms/amends), a `capture` backfill (with
  human confirmation), or the execution revision loop when the **user's**
  revision changes intent (`executing-rails-plan` R1 classification).
  Never silently rewritten by an implementor — every amendment traces to an
  explicit user decision.
- **`## Shape`** / **`evaluations:`** / `status:` — Agent-maintained at the
  defined write points: brainstorm (seeds the doc: `status: shaping`, rough
  Shape), planner (Shape; `status: planned` at final approval, except
  amendment plans leave `built` docs at `built`; during a lazy backfill,
  `status: built` plus `evaluations:` transcribed from already-existing
  specs), execution (close-out: `evaluations:`, `status: built`; revision loop:
  `evaluations:` sync when a fix moves or adds spec homes), maintainer agent
  (capture, update — including human-directed deprecation — lint, structural
  changes).
- **`## Provenance`** — **Append-only.** Never edit or delete an entry; a wrong
  entry gets a correcting entry.
- **`index.md`** — Stays in sync: every capability doc listed with a one-line
  outcome and status; update in the same change that adds or renames a doc. The
  line reads **`<name> — <status> — <outcome>. Owns: <models or resources>.`**
  The outcome must **discriminate** — enough that a reader deciding between two
  capabilities can tell them apart without opening either — and `Owns:` is the
  grep bridge for capabilities whose filename is not a resource name (§ Style).
  This is the tree's whole discovery surface; a vague line is how a duplicate
  capability gets created.

  ```markdown
  - `comment_threads` — built — readers reply to comments and see them nested. Owns: Comment.
  - `billing_plan_changes` — built — owners switch plan mid-cycle with proration. Owns: Subscription, Invoice.
  ```

## Capability doc format

Frontmatter carrying intent and evaluations, then two fixed H2 sections in this
order — fixed order is what makes docs traversable without search:

```markdown
---
status: shaping | planned | built | deprecated
intent:
  - id: I1
    clause: A reader can reply to any comment.
    evaluations:
      - spec/system/comment_threads_spec.rb
  - id: I4
    clause: Threads never nest deeper than 3 levels.
    superseded_by: [ I5, I6 ]
    superseded_on: 2026-09-18
---
# Capability name

## Shape
## Provenance
```

There is **no `## Intent` or `## Evaluations` section** — the clause and the
specs that prove it are one record in frontmatter, so a clause list and an
evaluation table cannot fall out of step. There is **no `specs:` key** either;
`agent_harness_rails evals` derives the reverse index from every clause's `evaluations`.

The key is **`superseded_on:`**, not `on:` — YAML resolves a bare `on` to the
boolean `true`, so an `on:` key silently loses its date.

Skeletons: `agent_harness_rails/skills/maintaining-primitives/references/templates.md`.

### Intent clauses

- **`id:`** — stable (`I1`, `I2`, …), assigned once, **never renumbered or
  reused**; provenance entries and old plans reference them.
- **`clause:`** — **one sentence, in outcome terms.** Write the quantifier you
  mean — *any*, *every*, *never*, *only* — because it is what the evaluations have
  to cover (`agent_harness_rails/rules/testing.mdc` § What counts as proving a
  clause).

#### What is and is not a clause

A clause is acceptable only when **all four** hold:

1. **Observable** — it names an outcome a user or a caller can see, not an
   internal arrangement.
2. **Falsifiable** — a spec can go red when it stops being true. "Replies appear
   nested under their parent" can fail; "threading feels natural" cannot.
3. **One behaviour** — one action, one outcome. No umbrella verb (*manage*,
   *handle*, *support*, *administer*), no `and` joining two different actions.
4. **Durable** — still worth stating after the implementation has been rewritten
   in a different shape.

The fourth settles most arguments, because it is the tree's whole premise: intent
is the primitive that **outlives generated code**, so anything a rewrite would
invalidate was never intent. Run it first — it is one question and it disposes of
architecture, technology choices, and task lists in a single move.

None of the following is a clause. Each has a home, and none of those homes is
`intent:`:

| Not a clause | Why | Where it belongs |
|---|---|---|
| "Comments use a `parent_id` self-join" | How the code is arranged; a rewrite invalidates it | `## Shape` |
| "Use Turbo Streams for replies" | A constraint on implementation, not a promise | `## Shape`, or `compilation.md` if app-wide |
| "Notifications move into a concern" | Nothing observable changes, so nothing could go red | `## Shape` if it leaves a constraint worth respecting; otherwise **nothing** — a refactor is git's business |
| "Threading feels natural", "the page is fast" | Not falsifiable — no spec can fail | Drop it, or restate as a measurable outcome |
| "Add a comments controller" | A work item, ephemeral by design | The plan's task list |
| "A member can manage their comments" | Several promises in one sentence; no example proves the whole | Split per action (below) |
| "Blank titles are rejected" | Rails' promise, not the app's | Nothing |
| "Renders the reply form" | The suite transcribed into the tree; a clause is a promise, not an example | The spec |

**Splitting an umbrella:**

| Instead of | Write |
|------------|-------|
| A member can manage their comments | A member can post a comment / can edit their own comment / can delete their own comment |
| Billing handles plan changes correctly | An owner can change plan mid-cycle / A proration line appears on the next invoice |

Granularity is the cheapest lever on the whole suite: a focused clause is proven
by a focused example at the layer that owns it, while a broad one has no home but
a wide system spec — which fights the budget and the Five Gates
(`agent_harness_rails/rules/testing.mdc`) and lands on the ~3-evaluation tripwire
below. **A clause you cannot prove without a journey spec is usually a clause
that has not been split yet.**

**The other bound — a clause is not a spec.** Scope words *sharpen* a clause,
they do not split it: "Only the author can edit an article" is one promise even
though proving it takes a permit case and a deny case at two layers. If a split
yields clauses no user would state separately, or that differ only by which layer
proves them, it has gone too far — that is transcribing the suite into the tree,
and the ~10-clauses-per-capability ceiling is what says so. Split by what the
product promises, never by what one example can assert.

#### Lifecycle

- **Superseding:** add `superseded_by: [ I5 ]` and `superseded_on: YYYY-MM-DD`
  to the old clause, and add the new clause with a **new** id. Never delete the
  old entry — tombstones are how a future agent learns a cap existed, why, and
  why it went. `retired_on:` covers a clause withdrawn with no replacement.
- **Draft stage:** clauses not yet referenced by any approved plan may simply
  be edited in place; the supersession mechanics above bind once a plan
  referencing them is approved.

### Shape

Deltas only — constraints specific to this capability that are **not**
derivable from the harness rules and **not** stated in `compilation.md`. Link,
don't repeat.

### Evaluations

Each **active** clause carries `evaluations:` — the RSpec files that prove it.
System specs are the browser evaluations. The named spec file must carry a
matching **`intent:` metadata tag** (below); a path alone is a claim, the tag is
the proof.

**The row has to be earned, not just filled.** `agent_harness_rails evals`
checks that the link is well-formed — clause exists, file exists, tag present —
and that is the whole of what a parser can settle. Whether the tagged examples
would actually go red if the clause stopped being true is judgment, and it is
the part the record lives or dies by: a clause with a green row that proves
nothing is worse than a bare clause, which at least fails honestly. The test and
its three common failures — asserting the affordance instead of the outcome,
proving one case where the clause claims a quantifier, proving it at a layer
that cannot hold it — are in
**`agent_harness_rails/rules/testing.mdc`** § What counts as proving a clause.

A clause therefore often lists **several files across several layers** — the
permission in a policy spec, the denial in a request spec, the journey in a
system spec. That is coverage of one clause, not duplication of one test; the
ownership table in `agent_harness_rails/rules/testing.mdc` makes it mandatory for
anything with an authorization side, since policy logic and its HTTP gate have
different homes by rule.

The brake on that is **necessity**: an evaluation you could delete while leaving
the clause fully proven is padding, and comes off the row. Rows are cheap to
lengthen and a long one reads as thorough, which is exactly why the check has to
be explicit — every listed file earns its place or goes.

Coverage is a **`built`-status obligation, and it is total**: every active
clause on a `built` doc names a spec, or `agent_harness_rails evals` fails.
`shaping` and `planned` docs for new work have none by design.

There is **no way to annotate a clause into being excused**. A built capability
whose behaviour nothing proves is a gap in the capability, not a nuance of the
record — and a marker that turns the gap into a passing run gets reached for
exactly when it should not be. Either write the spec, or the clause is not yet
`built`: split it out, leave the doc `planned`, or say plainly in provenance
that the behaviour shipped untested and expect a red run until it isn't.

One state does not fail, and it is about the branch rather than the capability:
a **`built` doc mid-amendment** — its latest provenance event is `planned`, so
an approved plan has landed ahead of its code. Its new clauses are legitimately
uncovered until close-out, and close-out is what clears the warning.

### Tagging the proof

The spec example carries the clause id as RSpec metadata:

```ruby
it "shows replies nested under their parent", intent: "comment_threads#I2" do
```

The capability is the doc's filename without `.md`. One example may carry
several clauses as a list — `intent: %w[comment_threads#I2 comment_threads#I3]`
— and a clause proven by four examples is tagged on all four.

**On the example, never on the group.** A tag on a `describe`, `context`, or
`shared_examples` is an offence — from `agent_harness_rails evals` as
`tag/misplaced`, and from the `AgentHarnessRails/IntentTag` cop at lint time:

```ruby
# bad — one line standing in for whatever the group happens to contain
describe "threading", intent: "comment_threads#I2" do
  it "nests replies" do
  ...

# good — the tag names the example that actually proves the clause
it "nests replies under their parent", intent: "comment_threads#I2" do
```

The group form looks tidier and is the one form that can go on lying: delete
the example the tag was really standing for and its siblings keep the group
green, so `rspec --tag` still passes and the clause still claims a proof that
no longer exists. Per-example tags cost a repeated line and buy the loud break
— when the last tagged example goes, the clause fails as unproven, which is the
whole point of the mechanism.

Metadata rather than a comment, so the same tag runs the proof:

```bash
bundle exec rspec --tag 'intent:comment_threads#I2'
```

Renaming an example cannot break the link; deleting it breaks it loudly.

### Checked mechanically

```bash
agent_harness_rails evals                    # from the app root; exits 1 on findings
```

It checks clause ↔ evaluation and nothing else: unproven built clauses, evaluations
naming a file that does not exist or does not carry the tag, tags naming a clause
that is absent or superseded, tags sitting on a group rather than an example,
duplicate ids, dangling supersessions, capability docs nested in a subdirectory,
and docs still in the retired prose format. Everything below — sizes, index sync, provenance
style — is judgment, and stays with the `lint` operation in `maintaining-primitives`.

### Provenance

One line per **event** (shaped, planned, built, amended, revised, refactored,
backfilled, confirmed, deprecated) — not per edit. Each line: date, what happened, link to the plan or PR, and rejected
alternatives or knowingly accepted debt when there were any. Multi-line entries
are a lint finding — the section must stay cheap to load and cheap to append.

**Every line earns its place with a future reader.** The test: a developer or
agent reading this doc in a year, deciding whether to change the code, is
better off for that line. Decisions, rejected alternatives, and accepted debt
pass. **Mid-task process does not** — how the work got done is git's job, not
provenance's:

| Keep out | Why |
|----------|-----|
| Review iteration counts, task counts, fix-pass tallies | Says how the sausage was made; changes no future decision |
| Who or what agent did the work, which skill or subagent ran | Not a property of the capability |
| Commands run, suites passed, lint status | `evaluations:` and CI are where proof lives |
| Progress narration ("started X", "continuing Y", "blocked then unblocked") | Only events land — a thing that happened and stayed true |
| Restated intent clauses or Shape bullets | Already above in the doc |

The same test governs the whole tree: **`intent:` clauses**, **`## Shape`**, and
**`compilation.md`** hold only what a future reader must know to change the
code correctly. Process, status chatter, and anything derivable from the code
or the harness has no home here.

## Size discipline (lint tripwires)

| Limit | Finding when exceeded |
|-------|----------------------|
| ~10 active intent clauses per capability | Split the capability — same instinct as a fat controller |
| ~3 evaluations on one clause | Two promises wearing one id (split the clause), or a padded row (drop the evaluations that prove nothing the others don't) |
| One sentence per clause, one line per provenance entry | Rewrite tighter |
| ~150 lines per capability doc | Fat provenance, a capability that should split, or Shape restating the harness |
| ~50 lines for `compilation.md` | It is describing the app, not constraining it |

## Style

- Prefer **relative links** inside the tree.
- Capability filenames follow the app's resource names (`agent_harness_rails/rules/naming.mdc`)
  so code → doc lookup works by grep — and because the filename is what an
  `intent:` tag names, a rename means retagging its examples. Choose the name as
  if it were permanent; at scale it effectively is.
- **`capabilities/` is flat, and the prefix is the namespace.** Not every
  capability is a resource: the ~10-clause tripwire splits `comments` into
  something like `comment_threads` and `comment_moderation`, and cross-cutting
  capabilities (authentication, search, notifications, audit logging) never owned
  a resource to begin with. Name those **`<domain>_<behaviour>`** —
  `billing_plan_changes`, `billing_invoices`, `billing_dunning`. A shared prefix
  sorts them together in a directory listing, makes `ls capabilities/ | grep
  billing` a working namespace query, and costs nothing structurally.

  Subdirectories are **not** the way to do this. `intent:` tags name a bare
  filename with no path in it, so a nested doc cannot be addressed by a tag —
  `agent_harness_rails evals` reports one as `doc/nested` rather than skipping it,
  which is what it used to do silently.
- Keep **Rails app code** out of this tree; it holds decisions and pointers,
  not application source.

## Relationship to Rails work

Edits here are **documentation** — they do not replace `agent_harness_rails/rules/testing.mdc`,
`implementing-rails-task`, or app implementation tasks. The specs the
`evaluations:` point at remain the executable truth; the frontmatter is the map,
and `agent_harness_rails evals` is what keeps the map honest.
